<Article slug="client-side-caching">
<p>A Redis cache hit avoids a database load, but the application still sends a request, waits for a reply and decodes it. If the same process asks for the same answer repeatedly, it could keep a copy in its own memory and avoid that path too.</p>
<p>Now there are three places to follow: the authoritative source, shared Redis, and local application memory. A change at one layer does not instantly change the others. The next sequence starts with the value 12 already cached in two application processes. Follow it through a source change, a delayed invalidation and a lost connection.</p>
<CacheHierarchy />
<p>At “The source changes”, all three cached copies still hold 12. At “Delete the shared entry”, Redis is empty, yet both local copies remain. A notification must reach each process before it can discard its own value. B’s intervening read shows the cost of that gap: it gets a fast answer which is already stale.</p>
<p>This is a <strong>cache hierarchy</strong>. We will call the process-local cache <strong>L1</strong> and shared Redis <strong>L2</strong>. Those names describe their position in this request path, not hardware CPU caches. The extra level can remove substantial repeated work, provided its lifetime and invalidation rules fit the application.</p>

<h2>Redis client-side caching and CLIENT TRACKING</h2>
<p>Redis provides server-assisted <strong>client-side caching</strong> through <strong>tracking</strong>. In its default mode, Redis remembers the keys a tracked connection reads. When a key changes, Redis sends an invalidation to interested clients. The client discards dependent cached results; its next read can fetch a new result.</p>
<p>The invalidation identifies what became invalid. It does not carry a replacement application value. That distinction lets the client avoid fetching entries which nobody asks for again.</p>
<p>With the <strong>RESP3</strong> protocol, replies and invalidation pushes can share one connection. The server-side command is <code>CLIENT TRACKING ON</code>. A maintained client library can enable tracking and manage its local result cache together. The native lab uses Redis 8.2.2 and node-redis 6.3.0 with RESP3 and an explicitly bounded cache.</p>
<p>The first two lab reads both return 12. Redis’s command statistics show only one GET was executed; the client records one miss and one local hit. A separate connection then writes 15. The reader receives an invalidation, discards its local entry, and returns 15 on the next GET.</p>
<p>Dependencies belong to cached command results, not necessarily one local entry per Redis key. The lab caches both GET and STRLEN for the same key: two different results. Changing that key invalidates both. It also caches a missing GET result, then observes that creating the key invalidates the cached absence. This matters when sizing a cache or deciding what “two entries” means.</p>
<p>Tracking connects L1 to changes in Redis. It does not know that a row in your application database changed. You still need the source-to-Redis contract from <a href="/learn/databases/caching/invalidation/">cache invalidation and consistency</a>. Nor does tracking infer arbitrary dependencies of a locally rendered page or an object assembled from external services.</p>
<p>Key design still matters at every level. A local cache keyed too broadly can mix tenants or representations even if Redis’s own keys are correct. Prefer caching the well-defined Redis result through the client unless there is a clear reason to introduce another application-specific result cache.</p>

<h2>Invalidation delivery and connection loss</h2>
<p>The opening sequence separates execution from notification delivery. Redis can have completed a write while another process has not yet processed its invalidation. A local read during that interval can use the old copy. Server-assisted invalidation is therefore not a promise that every local read immediately after a remote write sees the latest value.</p>
<p>Decide whether that interval fits the application contract. Display data may allow a bounded age; a decision requiring current authoritative state should follow a path which establishes that state. Putting more invalidations into the design does not remove the need to state the guarantee.</p>
<p>A lost invalidation connection is more dangerous than a delayed message. The client no longer knows which changes it missed. Its existing local entries must be discarded before treating the new connection as a trustworthy basis for caching. Reconnection should start from an empty local cache, not revive the previous connection’s entries.</p>
<p>The lab fills an entry, then kills only that reader’s connection on its private Redis server. The pinned client clears its local cache when it detects the failure. The writer changes the value to 21; after an explicit reconnect, the reader fetches 21. This verifies detected-disconnect behaviour. It does not prove how quickly a silent network failure will be detected.</p>
<p>That detection boundary needs attention precisely because L1 hits can avoid all Redis traffic. An open-looking socket may not establish that invalidations are still arriving. Verify the client’s liveness mechanism and configure an acceptable detection interval, local lifetime or application age limit. A boolean “connected” flag alone is not a freshness argument.</p>
<p>After clearing a large local cache, many requests may reach L2 together. If many application processes lose their connections together, the effect multiplies. Keep the <a href="/learn/databases/caching/stampedes/">coalescing and refill controls</a> and <a href="/learn/databases/caching/failure-handling/">bounded failure paths</a> from earlier articles at this new boundary too.</p>

<h2>Pending reads and invalidation races</h2>
<p>Consider a custom client with separate data and invalidation connections. It sends GET, the server reads 12, and that reply is delayed. Another writer changes the key. The invalidation arrives first; the client deletes its local entry. Then the old GET reply arrives.</p>
<p>If the client stores every completed reply, it has just put 12 back after invalidating it. Deleting an entry was insufficient because there was no completed entry to delete yet.</p>
<PendingCacheRead />
<p>The guarded side records a pending operation before sending GET. Invalidation cancels that operation’s permission to populate the cache. When the reply arrives, the client checks that the pending token is still current before installing the result. A generation number is one way to express this; a cancellable pending entry is another.</p>
<p>Notice what remains unchanged: the original request can still receive 12. The guard prevents that result from becoming a reusable local entry. If the request itself needs a stronger guarantee, it needs additional validation or a bounded retry according to the application’s consistency contract.</p>
<p>This particular wire-order race requires separate connections. A single RESP3 connection preserves the order of its replies and pushes. Custom asynchronous processing must still preserve the meaning of invalidation when it schedules later cache installation. The installed node-redis implementation uses pending entries and checks their validity before retaining a completed result. Use a tested client rather than assuming a map plus an invalidation callback covers every ordering.</p>

<h2>Local TTLs and end-to-end freshness</h2>
<p>A local cache TTL and a Redis key TTL are separate mechanisms. The lab deliberately sets a local lifetime of 60 seconds, then reads a Redis key expiring after one second. Redis expiry generates an invalidation; the local copy is discarded, and the next read returns no value. This observation does not establish an exact upper bound on notification delivery time.</p>
<p>Even without a connection failure, independently restarting lifetimes can extend the age of an observation. Suppose Redis holds data observed at time 0, with a freshness deadline of 60 seconds. L1 receives that same value at time 55 and gives it a new 30-second TTL. At time 75, the local timer permits a hit, although the source observation has exceeded its original budget.</p>
<LayeredFreshness />
<p>Enforcing the original deadline changes the local expiry from 85 to 60. L1 gets at most five seconds of useful lifetime from that late copy. This is correct if the contract allows the observation to be used only until time 60: moving it closer to the reader did not make it newer.</p>
<p>Carry freshness metadata such as an original observation time and absolute validity deadline with the value when the application needs this bound. Check it when serving a local hit. The clocks and interpretation of that timestamp must be sufficiently aligned for the contract. A new local insertion time is not a substitute.</p>
<p>If the value is too old, do not blindly retry the same cached GET: it may return the same rejected object again. Discard or bypass the relevant cached result and follow a bounded reload or refusal path. L2 may itself contain an expired application observation even before its Redis TTL has removed the key.</p>
<p>Reading a value and its remaining TTL in separate operations can also race with a replacement. If correctness depends on their relationship, obtain a consistent pair or store the application deadline inside the value. The built-in client’s configurable TTL does not automatically enforce every application payload’s freshness contract.</p>
<p>A maximum age bounds age, not equality with the latest source. An observation can be within its permitted lifetime and still precede a recent source update. Choose the guarantee required by the operation rather than describing every accepted cache entry as “current”.</p>

<h2>Reuse, invalidation fan-out and memory</h2>
<p>Local caching pays when a process reuses an answer between changes. Reading a key once after every update offers little reuse: each update invalidates the previous copy before it can help again. The client still pays for retaining entries and processing notifications.</p>
<p>The next model repeats one cycle. An update invalidates the existing copies; each process then reads the key several times before the next update. Reduce the reads between updates to one.</p>
<LocalCacheReuse />
<p>With four processes and ten reads each, L1 reduces forty Redis reads to four. Thirty-six requests stay local, while four invalidations are delivered per cycle. With one read per process, there are still four Redis reads, plus invalidation work. The change rate and per-process reuse matter together.</p>
<p>Adding application processes also adds copies. A hot key shared across many processes can be well suited to local reads, but its updates may fan out widely. Scaling the application can increase the minimum Redis reads, invalidations and memory even if the total end-user request rate stays constant. Measure the workload at the process level, not only as a global popular-key count.</p>
<p>The server must retain tracking metadata too. Default tracking limits its bookkeeping and may invalidate a tracked key to release that metadata even if the value did not change. An invalidation means “discard this cached dependency”, not necessarily “a business value changed”.</p>
<p>In <strong>broadcast mode</strong>, clients subscribe to key prefixes and receive matching invalidations even for keys they have not read. This avoids the per-read-key tracking table but still needs prefix/subscriber metadata and notification work. Our raw-protocol lab observes an invalidation for an unread matching key. That experiment is separate from node-redis’s integrated local-cache configuration, which does not expose every tracking mode.</p>
<p>Other protocol options let a client opt selected reads in or out, or suppress invalidations caused by its own connection with <code>NOLOOP</code>. These are client implementation choices, not free optimisations. Suppressing a notification is safe only if the client updates or discards all affected local results correctly itself.</p>
<p>Bound local storage explicitly. The lab uses <code>maxEntries: 2</code> to make eviction observable and retains two results after reading a third. Entry count is not a byte limit, and one result can be unexpectedly large. Include decoded values, cache metadata, copied return values and garbage collection in application memory measurements. Redis’s MEMORY USAGE describes Redis allocations; it is not a measurement of the application runtime’s heap.</p>
<p>Each process has its own capacity and access pattern. A shared cache can have a high hit rate while a newly started process’s local cache is cold. Process churn and rolling deployments therefore change the value of L1. Test these transitions instead of measuring only a long-lived, fully warmed process.</p>

<h2>Measure each cache boundary</h2>
<p>Redis no longer sees requests answered by L1. Suppose 100 application reads produce 80 local hits. Of the remaining 20 Redis lookups, 15 hit and 5 miss. Redis’s hit rate is 75%, while the combined path avoids the source for 95 of the 100 requests.</p>
<p>Those percentages have different denominators. A falling Redis hit rate can accompany a successful L1 if the easy repeated reads have disappeared from Redis’s workload. Record application requests, local hits and misses, Redis outcomes, source loads and errors separately. Treat a failed Redis lookup as a failure, not silently as an ordinary miss.</p>
<p>Also observe invalidation volume and processing delay, local evictions, memory, reconnect clearing and recovery load. A local hit which violates the freshness contract is not a successful optimisation, however quickly it returns.</p>
<p>The <a href="/learn/databases/caching/examples/redis-tracking-lab.zip" download>downloadable client-side caching lab</a> verifies the observations in this article with Node 24+, node-redis 6.3.0 and Redis 8.2.2. It starts a private Redis process, checks local hits against server command counts, exercises dependent-result and negative-result invalidation, then tests capacity, detected disconnection, expiry and raw prefix broadcasting. It cleans up its own process and temporary files.</p>
<p>Use L1 where repeated local reuse repays the extra copies and coherence work. Keep the source contract intact through every layer, and verify the actual client’s failure behaviour. Next, <a href="/learn/databases/caching/#collection">operating Redis caches</a> brings these decisions together in observation, security, diagnosis and controlled changes.</p>

